<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naab</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Naab is a river in the Upper Palatinate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and a left tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. It is formed by the meeting of the Waldnaab and Haidenaab and flows south for about 100 kilometres to join the Danube just above Regensburg. Its valley was a route between Bavaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and was held in part by the landgraves of Leuchtenberg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naab.jpg" alt="Naab" width="288" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pt:Usuário:nemracc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kallm%C3%BCnz_Die_Naab.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
